<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marquis Yi of Zeng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 433 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Zeng Hou Yi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marquis Yi of Zeng was a ruler of the small Zhou state of Zeng, identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>. His tomb at Leigudun near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suizhou">Suizhou</a>, excavated in 1978, held a set of 65 bronze bells, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lacquerware">lacquerware</a>, weapons and bamboo-slip writings. The bells, still playable, are among the most important finds for the history of Chinese music.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marquis-yi-of-zeng.jpg" alt="Marquis Yi of Zeng" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Zzjgbc at Chinese Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bianzhong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
